 for a Commonwealth offence, the court may declare a person to be a special witness in relation to the proceeding if satisfied that the person is unlikely to be able to satisfactorily give evidence in the ordinary manner because of:  (a) a disability; or  (b) intimidation, distress or emotional trauma arising from:  (i) the person’s age, cultural background or relationship to a party to the proceeding; or  (ii) the nature of the evidence; or  (iii) some other relevant factor.   Part IAD  Protecting vulnerable persons" ;
