concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19ae a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "19AE Persons already subject to recognizance release order"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-19ae ;
    values:originalText "(1) Where:  (a) a person is subject to a recognizance release order (in this section called the existing recognizance release order) made in respect of a federal sentence or federal sentences; and  (b) before the person is released under that order, the court imposes a further federal sentence on the person; this section applies.  (2) Where this section applies, the court must, after considering the relevant circumstances, including:  (a) the existing recognizance release order; and  (b) the nature and circumstances of the offence or offences concerned; and  (c) the antecedents of the person; do one of the following things:  (d) make an order confirming the existing recognizance release order;  (e) make a new recognizance release order in respect of all federal sentences the person is to serve or complete;  (f) where, as a result of the further federal sentence being imposed, the person is to serve or to complete a federal life sentence or federal sentences the unserved portions of which, in the aggregate, exceed 3 years and the court decides that it is appropriate to fix a non-parole period—fix a single non-parole period in respect of all federal sentences the person is to serve or complete;  (g) where the court decides that, in the circumstances, neither a recognizance release order nor a non-parole period is   Sentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB The fixing of non-parole periods and the making of recognizance release orders" ;
    skos:definition "(1) Where:  (a) a person is subject to a recognizance release order (in this section called the existing recognizance release order) made in respect of a federal sentence or federal sentences; and  (b) before the person is released under that order, the court imposes a further federal sentence on the person; this section applies.  (2) Where this section applies, the court must, after considering the relevant circumstances, including:  (a) the existing recognizance release order; and  (b) the nature and circumstances of the offence or offences concerned; and  (c) the antecedents of the person; do one of the following things:  (d) make an order confirming the existing recognizance release order;  (e) make a new recognizance release order in respect of all federal sentences the person is to serve or complete;  (f) where, as a result of the further federal sentence being imposed, the person is to serve or to complete a federal life sentence or federal sentences the unserved portions of which, in the aggregate, exceed 3 years and the court decides that it is appropriate to fix a non-parole period—fix a single non-parole period in respect of all federal sentences the person is to serve or complete;  (g) where the court decides that, in the circumstances, neither a recognizance release order nor a non-parole period is   Sentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB The fixing of non-parole periods and the making of recognizance release orders"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "702ef39864b00b1bf0b43a31fcaf6ec2300d39526d4f290516af7291b764cded" ;
    cof:pageNumber "248"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19ae-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19ae-ss-2 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
