doc:sec-19af-ss-2 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "19AF(2) Non-parole period or pre-release periods not to exceed" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(2) This section does not restrict the length of the non-parole period or the pre-release period in respect of a life sentence or sentences that include such a sentence." ;
    cof:pageNumber "249"^^xsd:integer .

concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19ag a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "19AG Non-parole periods for sentences for certain offences"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-19ag ;
    values:originalText "(1) This section applies if a person is convicted of one of the following offences (each of which is a minimum non-parole offence) and a court imposes a sentence for the offence:  (b) a terrorism offence;  (c) an offence against Division 80 (other than Subdivision CA) of the Criminal Code;  (d) an offence against subsection 91.1(1) or 91.2(1) of the Criminal Code. Note: A sentence for a minimum non-parole offence is a federal sentence, because such an offence is a federal offence.  (2) The court must fix a single non-parole period of at least 3/4 of:  (a) the sentence for the minimum non-parole offence; or  (b) if 2 or more sentences have been imposed on the person for minimum non-parole offences—the aggregate of those sentences. The non-parole period is in respect of all federal sentences the person is to serve or complete.  (3) For the purposes of subsection (2):  (a) a sentence of imprisonment for life for a minimum non-parole offence is taken to be a sentence of imprisonment for 30 years for the offence; and  (b) it does not matter:  (i) whether or not the sentences mentioned in that subsection were imposed at the same sitting; or  (ii) whether or not the convictions giving rise to those sentences were at the same sitting; or  (iii) whether or not all the federal sentences mentioned in that subsection are for minimum non-parole offences.  (4) If the person was subject to a recognizance release order, the non-parole period supersedes the order.   Sentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB The fixing of non-parole periods and the making of recognizance release orders" ;
    skos:definition "(1) This section applies if a person is convicted of one of the following offences (each of which is a minimum non-parole offence) and a court imposes a sentence for the offence:  (b) a terrorism offence;  (c) an offence against Division 80 (other than Subdivision CA) of the Criminal Code;  (d) an offence against subsection 91.1(1) or 91.2(1) of the Criminal Code. Note: A sentence for a minimum non-parole offence is a federal sentence, because such an offence is a federal offence.  (2) The court must fix a single non-parole period of at least 3/4 of:  (a) the sentence for the minimum non-parole offence; or  (b) if 2 or more sentences have been imposed on the person for minimum non-parole offences—the aggregate of those sentences. The non-parole period is in respect of all federal sentences the person is to serve or complete.  (3) For the purposes of subsection (2):  (a) a sentence of imprisonment for life for a minimum non-parole offence is taken to be a sentence of imprisonment for 30 years for the offence; and  (b) it does not matter:  (i) whether or not the sentences mentioned in that subsection were imposed at the same sitting; or  (ii) whether or not the convictions giving rise to those sentences were at the same sitting; or  (iii) whether or not all the federal sentences mentioned in that subsection are for minimum non-parole offences.  (4) If the person was subject to a recognizance release order, the non-parole period supersedes the order.   Sentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB The fixing of non-parole periods and the making of recognizance release orders"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "b0d7a1d0527b790543ee10f3a5a6c803e7c2d207c526c27e8f839a956a6caf9a" ;
    cof:pageNumber "250"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19ag-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19ag-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19ag-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19ag-ss-4 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <crimes-act-1914.pdf> ;
    dc:source "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.250" .
doc:sec-19ag a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "19AG Non-parole periods for sentences for certain offences" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "(1) This section applies if a person is convicted of one of the following offences (each of which is a minimum non-parole offence) and a court imposes a sentence for the offence:  (b) a terrorism offence;  (c) an offence against Division 80 (other than Subdivision CA) of the Criminal Code;  (d) an offence against subsection 91.1(1) or 91.2(1) of the Criminal Code. Note: A sentence for a minimum non-parole offence is a federal sentence, because such an offence is a federal offence.  (2) The court must fix a single non-parole period of at least 3/4 of:  (a) the sentence for the minimum non-parole offence; or  (b) if 2 or more sentences have been imposed on the person for minimum non-parole offences—the aggregate of those sentences. The non-parole period is in respect of all federal sentences the person is to serve or complete.  (3) For the purposes of subsection (2):  (a) a sentence of imprisonment for life for a minimum non-parole offence is taken to be a sentence of imprisonment for 30 years for the offence; and  (b) it does not matter:  (i) whether or not the sentences mentioned in that subsection were imposed at the same sitting; or  (ii) whether or not the convictions giving rise to those sentences were at the same sitting; or  (iii) whether or not all the federal sentences mentioned in that subsection are for minimum non-parole offences.  (4) If the person was subject to a recognizance release order, the non-parole period supersedes the order.   Sentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB The fixing of non-parole periods and the making of recognizance release orders" ;
    cof:pageNumber "250"^^xsd:integer .

