doc:sec-19al-ss-1a a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "19AL(1A) Release on parole—making of parole order" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(1A) If the Attorney-General does not, under subsection (1), make, or refuse to make, a parole order for a person before the end of the non-parole period referred to in that subsection, the Attorney-General must, as soon as practicable after the end of that period, make, or refuse to make, a parole order for the person." ;
    cof:pageNumber "254"^^xsd:integer .

concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19al-ss-2 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "19AL(2) Release on parole—making of parole order"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-19al-ss-2 ;
    values:originalText "(2) If the Attorney-General refuses to make a parole order for a person under subsection (1) or (1A), paragraph (b) of this subsection, or subsection (2A), (6) or (7), the Attorney-General must:  (a) give the person a written notice, within 14 days after the refusal, that:  (i) informs the person of the refusal; and  (ii) includes a statement of reasons for the refusal; and   Sentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB Conditional release on parole or licence  Division 5  Section 19AL  Crimes Act 1914  Compilation date: 04/06/2026  (iii) sets out the effect of paragraph (b) of this subsection; and  (b) reconsider the making of a parole order for the person and either make, or refuse to make, such an order, within 12 months after the refusal. Note: See subsection (5) if the person is subject to a State or Territory sentence." ;
    skos:definition "(2) If the Attorney-General refuses to make a parole order for a person under subsection (1) or (1A), paragraph (b) of this subsection, or subsection (2A), (6) or (7), the Attorney-General must:  (a) give the person a written notice, within 14 days after the refusal, that:  (i) informs the person of the refusal; and  (ii) includes a statement of reasons for the refusal; and   Sentencing, imprisonment and release of federal offenders  Part IB Conditional release on parole or licence  Division 5  Section 19AL  Crimes Act 1914  Compilation date: 04/06/2026  (iii) sets out the effect of paragraph (b) of this subsection; and  (b) reconsider the making of a parole order for the person and either make, or refuse to make, such an order, within 12 months after the refusal. Note: See subsection (5) if the person is subject to a State or Territory sentence."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "253d87bb4c3b680258d38e2b01e15615e811d351640f42c53e9eda44c78c71ad" ;
    values:partOf concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-19al ;
    cof:pageNumber "254"^^xsd:integer ;
    skos:note "The Attorney-General must reconsider the making of a parole order for a person within 12 months after refusal." ;
    dc:description "written notice with reasons and effect of refusal" ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
